  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bangladesh - Cyclone</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cyclone Rema hit Bangladesh on May 2024. Federation-Wide Emergency Appeal.
The severe cyclonic storm Remal made landfall near the Mongla and Khepupara coasts in Bangladesh and West Bengal in India at approximately 8:00 PM local time on 26 May 2024.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Federation-Wide Emergency Appeal aims to meet the immediate needs of 500,000 people in the eight districts most affected by Cyclone Remal.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The lack of access to adequate shelter due to damage or destruction has left many people in open spaces, their damaged houses, or taking refuge in the homes of friends and neighbours. Additionally, 93 per cent of people&#x2019;s livelihoods have been disrupted, plunging them into a state of financial uncertainty and instability. The monsoon season has already started in Bangladesh, and the heavy rainfall is expected to result in additional suffering for those affected, including exposure to disease outbreaks., Assistance focus through the provision of cash assistance, safe drinking water, sanitation, livelihoods, shelter, health, and disaster risk reduction support,</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The most impacted districts are Khulna, Bagerhat, Satkhira, Patuakhali, Pirojpur, Barguna, Jhalokathi and Bhola.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The storm surge, accompanied by extremely heavy rainfall, caused flooding of 5-8 feet in the coastal districts
Cyclone Remal affected roughly 4.6 million people in 19 districts, and at least 16 people died across seven districts. Approximately 800,000 people were evacuated to 9,424 evacuation shelters across 19 districts
Following the devastation caused by Cyclone Remal, the people worst affected are currently unable to meet their basic needs and require urgent humanitarian assistance</narrative>
  </description>
